Part II: Critical Architectural Problems
Problem 1: You Built a Monolith Disguised as Modules
Your architecture document reveals a system where the Android client is responsible for far too many concerns simultaneously: prompt construction, tool execution, context management, memory retrieval, PII filtering, provider orchestration, command routing, screen capture, audio control, and calendar management. These all live in the same Android application process.

This is your single biggest structural problem. It means that a bug in your audio controller can theoretically crash your AI reasoning pipeline. It means testing any one component requires standing up the entire application. And it means your server-side agent and client-side agent have unclear ownership boundaries — who decides what to do, the phone or the server?

The professional approach is a clean separation into three tiers. The first tier is a thin Android client that handles only UI rendering, local fast-path commands (regex-based), voice input/output, and screen capture. It should know nothing about prompt engineering or provider selection. The second tier is an agent service (your server, or a local PC) that owns all reasoning: prompt construction, tool selection, memory retrieval, provider orchestration, and execution planning. The third tier is external services: LLM providers, Tavily search, Google Calendar API, and your Supabase vector store. The Android client communicates with the agent service via a single protocol (your SSE stream is actually a good choice here), and the agent service communicates with external services. The phone should be a "dumb terminal" for AI decisions and a "smart terminal" for local device actions only.

Problem 2: Your Tool Design Violates the Single-Responsibility Principle
You have 9 "consolidated tools," and several of them are doing too many unrelated things. Your UniversalSearchTool handles web search, note search, AND semantic recall. Your SmartyCoreTool handles storing facts, retrieving memories, analyzing behavioral patterns, AND consolidating daily insights. Your KnowledgeMasterTool handles CRUD operations, archiving, summarizing, AND category management.

Anthropic's own engineering guidance (published in their "Writing Effective Tools for Agents" post) is explicit about this: "Make sure each tool you built has a clear, distinct purpose." They also warn that "too many tools or overlapping tools can distract agents from pursuing efficient strategies."

The issue isn't that consolidated tools are always wrong — Anthropic actually recommends consolidation in some cases (like a schedule_event tool that internally calls list_users, list_events, and create_event). The issue is that your consolidation boundaries are wrong. You're grouping by domain entity (Notes, Search, Time) rather than by workflow intent. A note search and a web search are completely different workflows, but you've merged them into one tool because they both have "search" in the name.

The professional approach follows Anthropic's recommendation: consolidate around high-impact workflows, not around domain entities. You'd want tools like search_and_summarize (one tool call that searches, retrieves, and produces a summary), capture_to_notebook (captures screen/voice/text and creates a structured note), schedule_and_remind (creates a calendar event and sets a reminder), and research_topic (orchestrates multiple web searches and synthesizes). Each tool should handle one complete user workflow, not one CRUD domain.

Problem 3: TOON Is Solving the Wrong Problem
Your TOON (Token-Oriented Object Notation) format strips quotes and whitespace from JSON to save tokens. This was a reasonable instinct in 2023, but it's the wrong optimization target today for two reasons.

First, modern LLMs are trained on JSON. They've seen billions of JSON examples in their training data. When you create a novel format the model has never seen, you're actually making the model work harder to parse and produce it, which increases error rates and latency. The tokens you save on formatting you lose on the model needing more reasoning tokens to handle your custom format.

Second, the real token cost isn't in the format — it's in what you're sending. If you're passing 8,000 tokens of irrelevant note content to answer a simple question, switching from JSON to TOON saves maybe 200 tokens. The 8,000 tokens of irrelevant content is the actual problem. Anthropic's tools guidance demonstrates this clearly: they recommend implementing response_format enum parameters (concise vs. detailed) so the agent itself can choose the verbosity level it needs.

The professional approach is to use standard JSON for all structured communication (the model knows it natively, every tool in every ecosystem parses it, and debugging is trivial), and instead optimize the content itself. Implement pagination, filtering, and truncation in your tools. Let the agent request concise or detailed responses. This is what Anthropic does with Claude Code, where they cap tool responses at 25,000 tokens by default.

Problem 4: Your CO-STAR Framework Is Over-Engineering Prompts
CO-STAR (Context, Objective, Style, Tone, Audience, Response) is a pedagogical framework for teaching people about prompt structure. It's useful as a mental model. But you've reified it into a literal code architecture — COSTARPromptBuilder.kt — that constructs prompts by mechanically filling in each section. This creates several problems.

The prompt becomes bloated because you're including "Audience: High-performance individual valuing precision" in every single API call, which wastes tokens on information the model doesn't need to see repeatedly. The sections create artificial boundaries that prevent natural prompt flow. And the dynamic injection of "Current State: Time, date, number of notes/categories" into every prompt is wasteful — most of that context is irrelevant to most queries.

The professional approach to prompt engineering is described well by Anthropic: start with the simplest prompt that works, then add complexity only when evaluation shows it's needed. Your system prompt should be a single, well-crafted paragraph that establishes Smarty's identity and core behaviors. Context should be injected selectively — only include note counts when the user is asking about notes, only include the time when the query is time-relevant. Style and tone instructions should be minimal and embedded naturally ("Be concise and direct" is better than a whole STAR section).

Problem 5: Your Context Management Is Naive
Your ContextManager has two modes: pass everything (for models like Gemini 1.5 that support huge context windows) and compress (stubbed, not implemented). This binary approach misses the entire field of context engineering.

The problem with "pass everything" is not just token cost — it's attention dilution. Even models with 1M+ context windows perform worse when irrelevant information is included, because the model's attention gets spread across noise. Research consistently shows that retrieving the right 2,000 tokens beats dumping 100,000 tokens of "everything" into the context.

The professional approach involves several strategies working together. First, implement a genuine RAG pipeline where you embed the user's query, search your vector store, and inject only the top-k most relevant results. You already have the Supabase/pgvector infrastructure — use it for conversation history too, not just long-term memory. Second, implement context windowing: keep the most recent 3-5 conversation turns in full, summarize older turns into compressed form, and drop anything older than a session boundary. Third, implement relevance filtering: before including any context (notes, screen content, memories), score its relevance to the current query and only include items above a threshold.

Problem 6: Your Memory System Lacks Structure
Your memory system stores "behavioral insights" and "long-term user preferences" in a vector store, but there's no taxonomy or structure to what gets stored. The ConversationSummarizer strips PII and compresses chats into "abstract behavioral insights," but there's no distinction between factual memories ("user works at Company X"), preference memories ("user prefers dark mode"), episodic memories ("user discussed project Y on January 5th"), and procedural memories ("user always wants notes saved in the 'work' category").

The professional approach is to implement a structured memory taxonomy. Each memory entry should have a type (factual, preference, episodic, procedural), a confidence score (how certain are we this is still true), a decay factor (preferences change, facts may become stale), and explicit relationships to other memories. Your retrieval should weight by recency, relevance, and type. When the user asks about their work project, you want to retrieve factual and episodic memories, not preference memories.